#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "01f32313-178c-5626-91af-110907202416" :question/math-academy-id "e-4936" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =⟨9,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z-56 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/id #uuid "d92eb242-104f-55cd-82dc-7620e2f4e636" :question/math-academy-id "e-4937" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "aa7c3400-9bc0-5f3a-84e5-f3cc2bb87f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4938" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi,$ find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "8a9e1070-0ca0-5f24-ac89-999c9271a91b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4939" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z-124 & =0 \\\\ 8x+2y+7z+62 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57b52441-4abc-5fec-8dd4-43409d99cc73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y+2z-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317146} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf4c908-9846-58e4-945d-de793e39c0de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y+3z-15=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317147} {:answer/id #uuid "897a956c-d023-565a-89e6-f1c1dec226a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y+2z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317148} {:answer/id #uuid "53a79750-d4cb-5287-aa29-e0ad549c69c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y+z-17=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317149} {:answer/id #uuid "d48859ef-c554-5111-a8cb-ebfe1415602d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y+z-13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57b52441-4abc-5fec-8dd4-43409d99cc73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y+2z-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317146} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bb935a1-4b1f-51aa-ac5d-cc43a7a98010" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7afe98b9-885a-5ca1-b17c-6d52dba0b047" :question/math-academy-id "q-127964" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(5,2,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=3i-j+2k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨5,2,-1⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨3,-1,2⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =⟨5,2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =11 \\\\ 3x-y+2z & =11 \\\\ 3x-y+2z-11 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74e40a26-493d-5b63-b2a7-b0c0857a63c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-3y-z+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317153} {:answer/id #uuid "96600223-6ac2-5aae-af5f-d339a3bf95ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+3y-z-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317154} {:answer/id #uuid "8fad3f9f-dd27-58db-96a2-20d9a9edd43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+4y-z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317155} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd52b89-44ba-5ac6-aa3e-ee8390f035fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-4y-z+9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317156} {:answer/id #uuid "83c33c72-a071-596b-90b3-cb3ce07d269b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-4z-17=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fad3f9f-dd27-58db-96a2-20d9a9edd43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+4y-z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317155} :answer-field/id #uuid "39431669-f8b7-5624-822e-3855a0a51429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03ce23ca-0088-57fb-b4ca-21f59a96ca63" :question/math-academy-id "q-127967" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-2,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=3i+4j-k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-2,3,-1⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨3,4,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨-2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z-7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a8f8f7b-56ca-5584-9272-69e6c9f76c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x+7y-z-14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317160} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5117de-679d-538f-9e86-4eae8e794dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7x-2y-z+19=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317161} {:answer/id #uuid "df4880fe-ca7e-5b6c-9747-9b8ddeca17b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+7y-2z+15=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317162} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d0078b-abab-51a1-8889-1ef1e3db1390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x+7y+z-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317163} {:answer/id #uuid "169c2631-db82-5bc8-b752-9a670c42f487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+2y-7z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a8f8f7b-56ca-5584-9272-69e6c9f76c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x+7y-z-14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317160} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb4499d1-c776-5e0a-903c-a8697aadc09a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0724766-b916-5512-ba7c-b23eb1b74775" :question/math-academy-id "q-127971" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-2,1,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨-2,7,-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-2,1,-3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨-2,7,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ & =⟨-2,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ & =14 \\\\ -2x+7y-z & =14 \\\\ -2x+7y-z-14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd99359a-d1c3-5457-b5b6-6118cb4ec749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x+7y-10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317167} {:answer/id #uuid "311a1aac-dbb2-538c-b650-99bb37bef703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y+7z+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317168} {:answer/id #uuid "cef1874f-6ea3-57d7-afc7-3ffd29c4bde0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7x+7y+7z-20=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317169} {:answer/id #uuid "41b312ce-a5c6-513e-b632-bac4c864b0aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+7z+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317170} {:answer/id #uuid "40e26e1c-d4ed-58d5-9242-ce5a467ee39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x+7z+20=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40e26e1c-d4ed-58d5-9242-ce5a467ee39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x+7z+20=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317171} :answer-field/id #uuid "27f77d74-147d-58b0-9a6b-e200b5888d6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d90c80a-6e49-52b9-8044-4809ca79d408" :question/math-academy-id "q-128375" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,0,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=7-2t \\\\ y=7 \\\\ z=7t\\end{aligned}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨3,0,-2⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨-2,0,7⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨-2,0,7⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ & =⟨3,0,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ & =-20 \\\\ -2x+7z & =-20 \\\\ -2x+7z+20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb8e7568-047d-5e41-b63c-205aaff1af1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,1⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,4,-4⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317174} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0aba5a-fa9a-5bb1-887a-0103271ca435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,4,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317175} {:answer/id #uuid "7c11b77b-6e9b-500f-a0e8-cc24d11fa912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨-4,4,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317176} {:answer/id #uuid "a190fab2-2f20-5523-a7aa-681ffd89d3e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,2⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,4,2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317177} {:answer/id #uuid "ad939636-b869-5138-9191-4d02b30ce1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,-2,4⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f0aba5a-fa9a-5bb1-887a-0103271ca435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,4,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317175} :answer-field/id #uuid "c922b201-a92f-5395-b5f0-4c722b17bf84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cbb9ba9-50e9-5a94-a501-7a9ce40ae41e" :question/math-academy-id "q-128380" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $4x+4y-2z=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨4,4,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+4y-2z & =0 \\\\ 2z & =4x+4y \\\\ z & =2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=0.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,4,-2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb6deb97-f107-513f-bf32-dc40eb368827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-4y-6z+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317181} {:answer/id #uuid "e7578732-1ace-5b8a-8787-a9fafc50a085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-2y+6z+21=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317182} {:answer/id #uuid "3760cdfe-ae3b-5f58-9fd1-75cc52efa930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-2y-6z+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317183} {:answer/id #uuid "022a6212-0f64-54db-b3d3-2da1d509da81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-6y-2z+7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317184} {:answer/id #uuid "743c7d2e-4f2c-54de-98d2-fde03125c7eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-6y-2z+17=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3760cdfe-ae3b-5f58-9fd1-75cc52efa930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-2y-6z+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317183} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb782205-3c0e-5252-aaed-f4e705886e25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "47e868ac-1a14-516c-8ec1-ee1245087be0" :question/math-academy-id "q-128462" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(1,-2,3)$, $P(3,-1,4)$ and $Q(-1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-2,3⟩,\\,p=⟨3,-1,4⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,4⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-4i+2j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-4,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =⟨3,-1,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z-10 & =0 \\\\ 4x-2y-6z+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd320510-1075-5e34-8eeb-c578ca849a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317188} {:answer/id #uuid "6489f1d6-5bad-54b1-8041-e0b0f9e9e222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+24=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317189} {:answer/id #uuid "7d72cb60-ea74-5c35-8b5a-0618e41e1504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317190} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b80d53-2e9a-538d-9b63-efb1fb02a239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z-26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317191} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7a3741-4493-561e-8fef-e14d90b81345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c7a3741-4493-561e-8fef-e14d90b81345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317192} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b3cc707-a5f8-5ce6-958c-76852cfc90f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc6b7572-6525-5f0a-acdf-da49eecac498" :question/math-academy-id "q-330498" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-2,4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,-2,4⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =⟨1,-2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z+26 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9b75b5f-06ee-5229-8e16-27c73bbdf60b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y-8z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317195} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8b9fc7-a2d6-502a-8201-e30feb601861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y-8z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317196} {:answer/id #uuid "753bf9d6-d048-51d8-87d2-f52594ed23a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x+3y+z+7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317197} {:answer/id #uuid "484e48bc-60cc-51d2-9492-301c5ddf8787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y-8z-17=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317198} {:answer/id #uuid "c73653b0-5c36-5497-8312-db686249b3ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x+3y-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9b75b5f-06ee-5229-8e16-27c73bbdf60b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y-8z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317195} :answer-field/id #uuid "094797d8-27a1-513d-842a-71a70b6bd1ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2d89667-fd21-5f9c-8885-bbfc06a5277b" :question/math-academy-id "q-33164" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-4,3,0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=2i+5j-8k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-4,3,0⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨2,5,-8⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ & =⟨-4,3,0⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ & =7 \\\\ 2x+5y-8z & =7 \\\\ 2x+5y-8z-7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c7b0e9-84e8-5c9c-ba0f-06626ea8fc1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c7b0e9-84e8-5c9c-ba0f-06626ea8fc1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317202} :answer-field/id #uuid "f11793dc-6a93-59ef-8ce8-cbd1fa85f5f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317201} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5a9a64d-1ad8-5fd9-a7ff-e1842bd77ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5a9a64d-1ad8-5fd9-a7ff-e1842bd77ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317204} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a2f3885-c732-5ca3-999a-26db1ceab88e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317203} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca6ba3ad-28aa-5386-aca3-2fe84afb3130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca6ba3ad-28aa-5386-aca3-2fe84afb3130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317206} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8480187-671e-5702-b732-d23eb9de328e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf13035e-4955-5aba-9932-4690b97ac996" :question/math-academy-id "q-336112" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-1,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$x\\,-\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨1,-4,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨1,-4,2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩ & =12 \\\\ x-4y+2z & =12 \\\\ x-4y+2z-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044a9694-62fb-5f03-8007-970f514bf767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "044a9694-62fb-5f03-8007-970f514bf767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317209} :answer-field/id #uuid "04a6c207-05a6-5228-b400-733381a15420" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317208} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f62607d4-1442-5ef9-8b7a-3bd2c7ec9f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f62607d4-1442-5ef9-8b7a-3bd2c7ec9f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317211} :answer-field/id #uuid "9961ae62-52ea-5336-adfe-57d9aa50bc17" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317210} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5670ab7c-e406-5031-bf38-072fdd15f96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5670ab7c-e406-5031-bf38-072fdd15f96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317213} :answer-field/id #uuid "a54b7b95-0b84-533f-8468-da8c58bb569c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c44c8817-42e9-5666-a2ac-7eba0ad11be5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336229" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $5x+2z-8=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,$\n{{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨5,0,2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2z-8 & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0,$ we get $z=4.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,4)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,4⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,0,2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7e21967-6d1b-5c5c-8d31-3e14d78c0047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e21967-6d1b-5c5c-8d31-3e14d78c0047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317216} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03294cd-3a6c-57c1-aca2-77be95811ea9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317215} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b0a80e6-a672-5e23-b77e-27278611a956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b0a80e6-a672-5e23-b77e-27278611a956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317218} :answer-field/id #uuid "364f4454-90b1-58d8-a274-8823b7080b0d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317217} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "437a35b5-91c8-514a-a1c3-04b4cc566eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "437a35b5-91c8-514a-a1c3-04b4cc566eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317220} :answer-field/id #uuid "7570e8b2-5387-5f5f-96df-c85bb817b2eb" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65948fec-f588-54c4-b605-f521e0f23300" :question/math-academy-id "q-336241" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $12y-6z-30=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,$\n{{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨0,12,-6⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12y-6z-30 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-12y+30 \\\\ z & =2y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=-5.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-5)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-5⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-5⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,12,-6⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ad3710d-d21c-504c-9c3a-237bbcf5f839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ad3710d-d21c-504c-9c3a-237bbcf5f839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317223} :answer-field/id #uuid "730de4bb-eef3-50b3-bc8f-e7c6ffcb28be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3f2ff546-7761-5f3a-872c-3eb0b39a7e47" :question/math-academy-id "q-336381" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(1,1,-1)$, $P(3,4,2)$ and $Q(-1,2,0).$\n\n$\\,y\\,-\\,z\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,p=⟨3,4,2⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,2,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,3⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0i-8j+8k \\\\  & =⟨0,-8,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =⟨3,4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z+16 & =0 \\\\ y-z-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f761c95-9f32-5612-9e67-fa0df18faa72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f761c95-9f32-5612-9e67-fa0df18faa72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317226} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab4131f0-8a86-5a6d-9a7a-9c289d4385df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317225} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b52ffa4-2203-571f-bd8f-3a50604af33a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b52ffa4-2203-571f-bd8f-3a50604af33a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317228} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a0c38fd-9fd4-586f-8f30-60515009bbae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317227} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8280fec-4a53-5c0b-bb76-2e7f9a245174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8280fec-4a53-5c0b-bb76-2e7f9a245174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317230} :answer-field/id #uuid "7741f7f0-ea69-5bb6-a42d-31ee48de0e0b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ab26089b-6055-52a7-add7-60a0d368cfd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-336384" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(2,-2,1)$, $P(5,0,3)$ and $Q(1,1,-1).$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,-\\,11z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-2,1⟩,\\,p=⟨5,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,3⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-10i+4j+11k \\\\  & =⟨-10,4,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =⟨5,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17 \\\\ -10x+4y+11z & =-17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z & =17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z-17 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d98e1129-6a50-5a34-9723-8863b00d0cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-3z-12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317233} {:answer/id #uuid "27309f2a-a373-52da-970f-0437230bf223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y-3x+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317234} {:answer/id #uuid "12725b19-d038-5268-aac4-d559dca8e151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y-3z-12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317235} {:answer/id #uuid "d684d6a2-d32b-5d86-ab15-8939c7ea5771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3z-12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317236} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc37be2-5b98-545b-a93b-eea458752355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-3z+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d98e1129-6a50-5a34-9723-8863b00d0cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-3z-12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317233} :answer-field/id #uuid "30045e12-e54d-5049-b52f-525974764046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e8af381-829f-5aee-8c8f-5f0b283d5aea" :question/math-academy-id "q-336538" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,-2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{5}=\\frac{z+2}{-3},\\,y=4\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨3,-2,1⟩.$ From the Cartesian equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨5,0,-3⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨5,0,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ & =⟨3,-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨5,0,-3⟩ & =12 \\\\ 5x-3z & =12 \\\\ 5x-3z-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a2f592a-f2ae-5741-8d47-7969dbee99b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4y-2z+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317240} {:answer/id #uuid "4699547e-7d88-5c79-a05c-aa4be50457b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4y+z+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317241} {:answer/id #uuid "9763fa43-49a8-54ac-8327-c39ace4eca59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y+14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317242} {:answer/id #uuid "be4bd53d-fa87-5296-9a6e-87f6fbc468e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4y+z+14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317243} {:answer/id #uuid "725e3432-c3d0-5d91-a7ce-4c07a9ca263d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4y+14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "725e3432-c3d0-5d91-a7ce-4c07a9ca263d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4y+14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317244} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf0c30f9-503f-5f2d-a8e9-ee837e0cad11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8487193a-71df-5209-912c-39653fe410a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-336540" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-4,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=-2+t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=5\\end{aligned}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-4,1⟩.$  From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨1,4,0⟩.$  So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨1,4,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ & =⟨2,-4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,4,0⟩ & =-14 \\\\ x+4y & =-14 \\\\ x+4y+14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f61e67-d536-5694-b66a-9aa84d0ee15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 5y + 5z - 16 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117797} {:answer/id #uuid "07240827-a981-5f95-9c8e-38cdca43d4a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 5y + 5z + 16 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117798} {:answer/id #uuid "29052769-126a-5866-ba29-4d94c9d0440a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x - 2y + z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117799} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1aefd3-ac99-5205-83bf-751c14e9569a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x - 2y + z + 16 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117800} {:answer/id #uuid "69349a20-593e-5360-82ee-a5b6257317e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 5y + 5z = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f61e67-d536-5694-b66a-9aa84d0ee15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 5y + 5z - 16 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117797} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e6a3b15-d638-5466-a39d-2a8c03dfaae7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93f0f0ea-5609-5473-8b26-f85225442381" :question/math-academy-id "q-33912" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨5,2,1⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,3,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,0,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =3i-15j+15k \\\\  & =⟨3,-15,15⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =⟨1,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z-48 & =0 \\\\ x-5y+5z-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e65e2a5-4979-5ab2-9acf-68f15e9f61d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-y-2z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317247} {:answer/id #uuid "38dffdf1-e644-5aaa-b63e-e6af6c9fd7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317248} {:answer/id #uuid "41f418f9-fa65-521c-86aa-8bdf00e2e80a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-y-2z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317249} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1ba502-ab3e-5eae-8458-38a8e266b715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317250} {:answer/id #uuid "43158893-8356-5c63-8f19-44104e38a3c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e65e2a5-4979-5ab2-9acf-68f15e9f61d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-y-2z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317247} :answer-field/id #uuid "62c73c61-084f-598f-9a26-388093e5eff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08692043-b7cd-55e2-b86c-6bc1b3ef2402" :question/math-academy-id "q-33913" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(1,-1,1)$, $P(2,0,3)$ and $Q(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-1,1⟩,\\,p=⟨2,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-5i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-5,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 5x-y-2z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9283ff37-3e4f-5163-ad99-71efe40ad5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0, 1⟩) \\cdot ⟨ - 1,-1, 16⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117803} {:answer/id #uuid "ac70ffe8-eae8-556a-97d5-052b005eed8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0, 1⟩) \\cdot ⟨1,-1, 4⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117804} {:answer/id #uuid "bd828451-41f3-5243-bda4-b6fab3137715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0,-4⟩) \\cdot ⟨1,-1, 4⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117805} {:answer/id #uuid "02b09862-2f48-5ea4-bf9b-bda41a4c4028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0, 4⟩) \\cdot ⟨1,-1, 4⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117806} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5f9c12-c888-5da9-9418-2ad33e18b29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0,-1⟩) \\cdot ⟨1, 0, 4⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd828451-41f3-5243-bda4-b6fab3137715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r - ⟨0, 0,-4⟩) \\cdot ⟨1,-1, 4⟩ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117805} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cadd5d9-6b27-5ebe-b532-ee330506bfa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d949e3c-e35b-5754-bfa5-04230aa782a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-33923" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $x-y+4z+16=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨1,-1,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+4z+16 & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-4.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-4)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-4⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-4⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,4⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d089bd5-bc73-50b4-9789-cefe8e13dd73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y-2z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317254} {:answer/id #uuid "99b50b55-44c9-5578-806b-352c17204190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x+y+z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317255} {:answer/id #uuid "64cba4dd-9dc5-53ef-acae-98028b7c263b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y-2z-14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317256} {:answer/id #uuid "6e65ef75-b03c-52a9-aa96-50477a3fa84f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y-z-14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317257} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed7e429-d015-5de3-9015-5a55d26ad276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-z-14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64cba4dd-9dc5-53ef-acae-98028b7c263b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y-2z-14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317256} :answer-field/id #uuid "11e75fd7-4368-5648-a1cc-04738d2cfcc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a01c8d0c-708e-5a28-b9ae-1414f36e9dc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-70809" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,3,-1⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,2,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨3,2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =⟨2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =14 \\\\ 3x+2y-2z & =14 \\\\ 3x+2y-2z-14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "420c5cf1-e38b-555d-8f2d-5c1be19e580b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,2⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,0,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317261} {:answer/id #uuid "4f20ac7c-1521-57ab-85fc-0223dd47a1c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317262} {:answer/id #uuid "29ccc51c-602d-5160-ae13-f9b7861d4830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,1,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317263} {:answer/id #uuid "42fc3607-c2c4-5193-981f-41fe0c8bd591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,1,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317264} {:answer/id #uuid "eec27715-4299-5397-aac4-782ae832f15b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-2,-4⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42fc3607-c2c4-5193-981f-41fe0c8bd591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,1,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317264} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c8bc945-f185-55c7-ac41-b7612c981ac6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73c1d5d9-6a23-5438-8d9f-6779511254e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-70818" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x+y-2z-4=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨2,1,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z-4 & =0 \\\\ -2z & =-2x-y+4 \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-2)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,1,-2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
